<!doctype html>
<html xmlns:hta="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
<head>
<meta http-equiv="content-type" content="text/html; charset=utf-8">
<title>Ebiki</title>
<!--
  Ebiki start-up splash.

  Clicking the Desktop shortcut used to show NOTHING for several seconds: the
  shortcut runs launch-ebiki.vbs, which runs launch.ps1 hidden, which starts
  Anki, the dev server and Electron - all invisible - so the app "randomly
  opened a little bit afterwards" and in the meantime nothing said it was
  loading (not even the busy cursor other apps get). This window is that
  missing feedback: mshta.exe paints it in a few hundred ms, long before
  anything else exists.

  showintaskbar="no" is deliberate. mshta.exe is its own process, so a splash
  with showintaskbar="yes" gets WS_EX_APPWINDOW (measured) and Windows gives it
  a SECOND Ebiki button beside the pinned one - two identical icons for one
  app, which reads as "it opened something else". It cannot be made to share
  the pinned button instead: the taskbar groups by AppUserModelID, and setting
  one needs SetCurrentProcessExplicitAppUserModelID before the window exists,
  which an HTA has no way to call. So the duplicate is removed, not merged.
  Nothing is lost - the window itself is the feedback, and it opens focused and
  centred. NOTE: every attribute below has to stay a bare attribute; an HTML
  comment inside that tag is invalid and silently stops them applying, exactly
  like the ie=edge trap described further down.

  It closes as soon as the app window is actually on screen: electron/main.cjs
  touches <app>\.app-ready in its ready-to-show handler, and scripts/launch.ps1
  touches the same file on every path that never gets that far (no Electron,
  browser fallback, missing Node, crash). The hard timeout below is the last
  resort so a splash can never be left on screen by itself.
-->
<hta:application
  id="ebikiSplash"
  applicationname="Ebiki"
  icon="../ebiki.ico"
  border="none"
  caption="no"
  sysmenu="no"
  maximizebutton="no"
  minimizebutton="no"
  showintaskbar="no"
  innerborder="no"
  scroll="no"
  selection="no"
  contextmenu="no" />
<style>
  html, body { margin: 0; overflow: hidden; }
  body {
    background: #F2F5F8;
    color: #16232E;
    font-family: "Segoe UI", "Nunito", Tahoma, sans-serif;
    cursor: progress;
    border: 1px solid #DCE3EA;
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }
  /* Fixed content width, natural height: fitWindow() below measures this box
     and sizes the window to it, so nothing can be clipped. */
  .wrap { display: block; width: 348px; padding: 20px 26px 18px 26px; }
  .row { width: 100%; }
  /* Ebi is 221x126, NOT square: give the image a width and let the height
     follow it, or the pose comes out visibly squashed (a fixed 74x74 box did
     exactly that). No object-fit - the legacy rendering mode this HTA needs
     predates it. */
  .ebi { float: left; width: 100px; margin: 0 16px 0 0; }
  .ebi img { width: 100px; }
  /* The text is a COLUMN beside Ebi, not text flowing around him. A float only
     pushes lines aside while they are level with it: the moment the message grew
     past Ebi's height the next line wrapped back to the full width and started
     under his tail, so a two-line message came out with its last line hanging off
     to the left. Reserving the float's width (100 + 16) keeps every line in the
     same column, however long the message gets - and messages here are written by
     the launcher, so their length is not something this file can predict. */
  .col { margin-left: 116px; }
  .title { font-size: 21px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .2px; padding-top: 6px; }
  .sub { font-size: 12.5px; color: #62717F; margin-top: 4px; }
  .clear { clear: both; height: 18px; }
  .track {
    position: relative; height: 6px; border-radius: 4px;
    background: #E2E8EE; overflow: hidden;
  }
  /* Moved by script, not by CSS: an HTA only honors the caption="no" /
     border="none" attributes above in its LEGACY rendering mode, and asking for
     ie=edge to get @keyframes brings the title bar and window buttons back. A
     borderless splash is worth more here than a CSS animation, so the bar is
     stepped in JavaScript instead - which every mode supports. */
  .bar {
    position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; height: 6px; width: 38%;
    border-radius: 4px; background: #DF2540;
  }
  /* The update question lives HERE, not in a separate dialog. A WScript popup
     opened UNDERNEATH this window (it is a different process, and this one is
     shown first), so the launch just looked frozen for the whole timeout while
     an unseen question waited for an answer. One window, one thing to look at. */
  #ask { display: none; margin-top: 4px; }
  body.asking { cursor: default; }
  #ask button {
    font-family: inherit; font-size: 12.5px; font-weight: 700;
    padding: 7px 16px; margin-right: 8px; border-radius: 6px; cursor: pointer;
  }
  #ask .yes { background: #DF2540; color: #fff; border: 1px solid #C41F37; }
  #ask .no  { background: #fff; color: #62717F; border: 1px solid #DCE3EA; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="wrap" id="wrap">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="ebi"><img src="../public/assets/shrimp/6820-holeshrimp.png" alt=""></div>
      <div class="col">
        <div class="title" id="title">Starting Ebiki</div>
        <div class="sub" id="sub">Waking up Anki and the study server.</div>
        <div id="ask">
          <button class="yes" id="yesBtn" onclick="answer('yes')">Update now</button><button class="no" id="noBtn" onclick="answer('no')">Not now</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="clear"></div>
    <div class="track" id="track"><div class="bar" id="bar"></div></div>
  </div>

<script type="text/javascript">
// Size and place the window. The box is MEASURED, not assumed: a splash is one
// short line of text and one image, but this window can open on any screen and
// at any Windows scaling factor, and how mshta resolves a CSS pixel there is
// not something the page gets to see. So the layout is laid out at its natural
// size first, then the window is fitted to it and centred on the WORK AREA
// (taskbar excluded) - which keeps it correct on a 1366 laptop and a 4K panel
// alike, and means a bigger system font can never clip the text. Clamped to the
// screen so it can never end up wider than the desktop it sits on.
function fitWindow() {
  var wrap = document.getElementById('wrap');
  var W = Math.max(360, wrap.scrollWidth + 2);
  var H = wrap.scrollHeight + 2;
  if (W > screen.availWidth - 40) W = screen.availWidth - 40;
  if (H > screen.availHeight - 40) H = screen.availHeight - 40;
  try {
    window.resizeTo(W, H);
    window.moveTo(Math.max(0, (screen.availWidth - W) / 2), Math.max(0, (screen.availHeight - H) / 2));
  } catch (e) {}
}
fitWindow();

// The moving bar. Stepped by hand (see the note in the stylesheet) so it works
// in the legacy rendering mode a chrome-free HTA window requires.
var barPos = -40;
var bar = document.getElementById('bar');
window.setInterval(function () {
  barPos += 3.2;
  if (barPos > 102) barPos = -40;
  bar.style.left = barPos + '%';
}, 40);

// This file lives in <app>\scripts\, so the marker sits one folder up. Derived
// from our own URL rather than passed in, so the launcher stays path-relative.
var fso = new ActiveXObject('Scripting.FileSystemObject');
var htaPath = unescape(location.href).replace(/^file:\/+/, '').replace(/\//g, String.fromCharCode(92));
var appDir = fso.GetParentFolderName(fso.GetParentFolderName(htaPath));
var readyFile = fso.BuildPath(appDir, '.app-ready');

// A whole file as UTF-8 text ('' when missing or busy). ADODB.Stream, not FileSystemObject: FSO reads only
// ANSI or UTF-16, and the launcher's messages are translated (Chinese, Japanese...) and written as UTF-8.
function readUtf8(path) {
  try {
    if (!fso.FileExists(path)) return '';
    var st = new ActiveXObject('ADODB.Stream');
    st.Type = 2; st.Charset = 'utf-8'; st.Open(); st.LoadFromFile(path);
    var txt = st.ReadText(); st.Close();
    return txt.replace(/^﻿/, '');
  } catch (e) { return ''; }
}
// This window's text comes from the app's locale files, through their generated copy
// scripts/launcher-strings.json (npm run i18n:launcher). Language: applang.json (the app language) -> the
// Windows language -> English. This legacy-mode engine has no JSON object; the file is our own, so eval.
var L = {}, LF = {};
(function loadText() {
  var all = null, saved = null;
  try { all = eval('(' + readUtf8(fso.BuildPath(fso.GetParentFolderName(htaPath), 'launcher-strings.json')) + ')'); } catch (e) {}
  try { saved = eval('(' + readUtf8(fso.BuildPath(appDir, 'applang.json')) + ')'); } catch (e) {}
  if (!all || !all.langs) return;
  var sys = String(navigator.browserLanguage || navigator.systemLanguage || navigator.userLanguage || '').toLowerCase().split('-')[0];
  var lang = (saved && saved.lang && all.langs[saved.lang]) ? saved.lang : (all.langs[sys] ? sys : all.fallback);
  L = all.langs[lang] || {};
  LF = all.langs[all.fallback] || {};
})();
function T(key, english) { return L[key] || LF[key] || english; }
document.getElementById('title').innerText = T('ln_startingTitle', 'Starting Ebiki');
document.getElementById('sub').innerText = T('ln_wakingUp', 'Waking up Anki and the study server.');
document.getElementById('yesBtn').innerText = T('ln_updateNow', 'Update now');
document.getElementById('noBtn').innerText = T('ln_notNow', 'Not now');
fitWindow();
// scripts/launch.ps1 writes a one-line description of whatever it is doing right
// now into this file. Showing it is the whole point: a launch that pauses to
// check for an update, waits on the update question, or spends a minute running
// npm install is INDISTINGUISHABLE from an app that is just slow - which is
// exactly how someone sat through long start-ups for weeks without ever
// realising an update was waiting for him.
var statusFile = fso.BuildPath(appDir, '.app-status');
var lastStatus = '';

// The update question is ASKED IN HERE. It used to be a WScript.Shell popup from
// launch.ps1, which is a separate process shown after this window - so it opened
// UNDERNEATH the splash and the launch looked frozen for the whole timeout while
// an invisible question waited for an answer, which is exactly how people ended
// up running old builds for weeks. Two files carry the conversation:
//   .app-splash  - written here on load, so the launcher knows there is a window
//                  to ask in and can fall back to its own dialog if there isn't;
//   .app-answer  - written on click ('yes' / 'no'), which the launcher polls for.
var answerFile = fso.BuildPath(appDir, '.app-answer');
try { fso.CreateTextFile(fso.BuildPath(appDir, '.app-splash'), true).Close(); } catch (e) {}
var prompting = false;
var answeredPrompt = null;   // the question already answered: the status file still
                             // holds it for a moment after the click, and without
                             // this the next poll would ask it all over again

function answer(v) {
  if (!prompting) return;
  answeredPrompt = document.getElementById('sub').innerText;
  try { var f = fso.CreateTextFile(answerFile, true); f.Write(v); f.Close(); } catch (e) {}
  prompting = false;
  document.getElementById('ask').style.display = 'none';
  document.getElementById('track').style.display = 'block';
  document.getElementById('title').innerText = T('ln_startingTitle', 'Starting Ebiki');
  document.body.className = '';
  lastStatus = '';   // so the launcher's next status line is shown even if it repeats
  showStatus(v === 'yes' ? (promptYesText || T('ln_updating', 'Updating Ebiki. This can take a minute, please wait.')) : T('ln_starting', 'Starting Ebiki.'));
}

// title / yesText are optional (the ASK| form): the Anki update asks in this same window with its
// own heading and its own "working on it" line. Without them the original Ebiki wording is used.
var promptYesText = '';
function showPrompt(txt, title, yesText) {
  if (prompting) return;
  prompting = true;
  promptYesText = yesText || '';
  document.getElementById('title').innerText = title || T('ln_updateTitle', 'Update available');
  document.getElementById('sub').innerText = txt;
  document.getElementById('ask').style.display = 'block';
  document.getElementById('track').style.display = 'none';
  document.body.className = 'asking';
  fitWindow();
  try { window.focus(); } catch (e) {}
  // "Update now" is the preselected answer, so Enter takes the recommended (latest) choice.
  try { document.getElementById('yesBtn').focus(); } catch (e) {}
}

function readStatus() {
  try {
    if (!fso.FileExists(statusFile)) return '';
    // UTF-8 (the launcher writes translated text). "@key" is a message named by its key (launch-ebiki.vbs
    // writes one before any translation is loaded).
    var txt = readUtf8(statusFile).split(/\r?\n/)[0].replace(/^\s+|\s+$/g, '');
    if (txt.charAt(0) === '@') txt = T(txt.substring(1), '');
    return txt;
  } catch (e) { return ''; }   // being written to right now - try again next tick
}

function showStatus(txt) {
  if (txt === lastStatus) return;
  lastStatus = txt;
  // Fresh news means the launcher is alive and working, so the give-up timer
  // below measures SILENCE rather than total time. Without this, a launch that
  // legitimately takes longer than the cap (the update question, then a pull and
  // an npm install) would lose its splash halfway through and look like a crash.
  waited = 0;
  document.getElementById('sub').innerText = txt;
  // The window was fitted to the OPENING line; a longer message wraps, and
  // body{overflow:hidden} would quietly clip it.
  fitWindow();
}

var waited = 0;
var STEP = 250, LIMIT = 180000;  // give up after 3 min of SILENCE (see showStatus:
                                 // any new status line resets this), so an update
                                 // installed at launch can take as long as it needs

// The marker says "a window is listening": left behind after the silence cap (or Alt+F4), the launcher's
// next question went to a closed window, waited its full timeout invisibly, and the update was skipped.
function dropSplashMarker() { try { fso.DeleteFile(fso.BuildPath(appDir, '.app-splash'), true); } catch (e) {} }
window.onbeforeunload = dropSplashMarker;
function done() { dropSplashMarker(); try { window.close(); } catch (e) {} }

function tick() {
  waited += STEP;
  try { if (fso.FileExists(readyFile)) return done(); } catch (e) {}
  if (waited > LIMIT) return done();
  // A prompt holds the window open for as long as it takes: a question nobody
  // has answered yet must never be closed out from under the person reading it.
  // ...UNLESS the launcher has stopped asking. It waits a fixed time for an answer and then
  // carries on (writing its next status line); leaving the buttons up let a returning user
  // click "Update now", see "Updating Ebiki...", and get the old build with nothing updated.
  if (prompting) {
    var pending = readStatus();
    if (!pending || pending.substring(0, 7) === 'PROMPT|' || pending.substring(0, 4) === 'ASK|') {
      waited = 0; window.setTimeout(tick, STEP); return;
    }
    answeredPrompt = document.getElementById('sub').innerText;   // never re-ask this one
    prompting = false;
    document.getElementById('ask').style.display = 'none';
    document.getElementById('track').style.display = 'block';
    document.getElementById('title').innerText = T('ln_startingTitle', 'Starting Ebiki');
    document.body.className = '';
    lastStatus = '';
  }
  var status = readStatus();
  if (status.substring(0, 7) === 'PROMPT|') {
    var question = status.substring(7);
    if (question !== answeredPrompt) showPrompt(question);
  } else if (status.substring(0, 4) === 'ASK|') {
    // ASK|<title>|<status after Yes>|<question> (the question may itself contain no '|')
    var parts = status.substring(4).split('|');
    var q = parts.slice(2).join('|');
    if (parts.length >= 3 && q !== answeredPrompt) showPrompt(q, parts[0], parts[1]);
  } else if (status) showStatus(status);
  // Say something instead of looking stuck. Deliberately names NO single cause:
  // this used to claim "the first start takes a little longer", which is wrong
  // every time the wait is just a slower computer (or a busy one, or a cold
  // disk) rather than a first run. Re-fit afterwards - fitWindow() ran once at
  // load, against the SHORTER opening line, and body{overflow:hidden} would
  // quietly clip a message that now wraps to a second line.
  // Only when the launcher has not said anything more specific - a real status
  // (checking for updates, installing one, waiting on the update question)
  // always beats a generic "hang on".
  if (waited >= 12000 && !status) {
    showStatus(T('ln_almostThere', 'Almost there. First starts and slower computers take a bit longer.'));
  }
  window.setTimeout(tick, STEP);
}
window.setTimeout(tick, STEP);
</script>
</body>
</html>
